From 51b764376b10723493bda755eea604340a48864b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tengzhen <tengzhen.xiong@tuya.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 18:47:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] bluetooth: RTL8733BU legacy LE advertising and built-in BT
 bring-up

Add HCI_QUIRK_BROKEN_EXT_ADV and set it from the rtk_btusb probe. The
RTL8733BU claims HCI_LE_EXT_ADV, but driving BlueZ down the extended
advertising path on this combo does not produce a device the Tuya App or
nRF can find. The quirk masks HCI_LE_EXT_ADV out of hdev->le_features so
ext_adv_capable() is false and the legacy ADD_ADVERTISING path is used.

Alongside the quirk, set le_adv_min/max_interval to 100/120ms. Legacy
MGMT_OP_ADD_ADVERTISING ignores BlueZ's MinInterval, so the 1.28s default
made phones take a long time to discover the device.

Fix hci_schedule_adv_instance_sync() arming the rotation timer for a
single unlimited instance: duration defaulted to HCI_DEFAULT_ADV_DURATION
(2s) and adv_instance_expire then tore down LE Set Adv Enable, so the
device went quiet a couple of seconds after RegisterAdvertisement
returned OK. Only arm the expire work for real multi-instance rotation.

Boot time: build dwc2, the Rockchip USB2 PHY and rtk_btusb into the
kernel, with the controller's patch/config blobs linked in via
CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE (55KB). hci0 then exists at the end of kernel init
rather than only after userspace modprobes the driver, so the phone can
see the device much sooner. The driver Makefile hardcoded obj-m, which
silently produced a module even with CONFIG_BT_RTK_HCIBTUSB=y; honour the
tristate instead. The driver's optional /opt/bdaddr and
/opt/rtk_btconfig.txt reads happen before rootfs mount now, but this
board ships neither file.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm/configs/rk3506-alientek.config       |  17 ++++++++-
 .../bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/Makefile   |   6 +++-
 .../bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/rtk_bt.c   |  33 ++++++++++++++++++
 firmware/rtl8733bu_config                     | Bin 0 -> 14 bytes
 firmware/rtl8733bu_fw                         | Bin 0 -> 55236 bytes
 include/net/bluetooth/hci.h                   |  13 +++++++
 include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h              |   4 ++-
 net/bluetooth/hci_event.c                     |  21 +++++++++++
 net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c                      |  21 +++++++----
 9 files changed, 105 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 firmware/rtl8733bu_config
 create mode 100644 firmware/rtl8733bu_fw

diff --git a/arch/arm/configs/rk3506-alientek.config b/arch/arm/configs/rk3506-alientek.config
index 5272cf04c..6c6609a42 100644
--- a/arch/arm/configs/rk3506-alientek.config
+++ b/arch/arm/configs/rk3506-alientek.config
@@ -15,13 +15,28 @@ CONFIG_EXT2_FS_POSIX_ACL=y
 CONFIG_EXT2_FS_SECURITY=y
 CONFIG_EXT3_FS=y
 CONFIG_USB=y
+# Built-in (base defconfig has both =m): the RTL8733BU WiFi/BT combo hangs off
+# dwc2, so with them modular nothing enumerates until S03modules_init runs in
+# userspace, and hci0 only appears after that. Built-in, USB probes during
+# kernel init in parallel with UBI attach / rootfs mount, so rtk_btusb (still a
+# module, it needs firmware from the rootfs) finds the device already there.
+CONFIG_USB_DWC2=y
+CONFIG_PHY_ROCKCHIP_INNO_USB2=y
 CONFIG_MOTORCOMM_PHY=y
 CONFIG_FB=y
 CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE=y
 CONFIG_BT=y
 CONFIG_RFKILL=y
 CONFIG_BT_HCIUART=y
-CONFIG_BT_RTK_HCIBTUSB=m
+# Built-in so hci0 exists at the end of kernel init instead of only after
+# userspace modprobes it. The controller needs its patch + config blob at probe
+# time, which is before the rootfs is mounted, so both are linked into the
+# kernel via CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE below (55KB total). The driver's other two
+# file reads (/opt/bdaddr, /opt/rtk_btconfig.txt) are optional and this board
+# ships neither, so losing them at probe time costs nothing.
+CONFIG_BT_RTK_HCIBTUSB=y
+CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE="rtl8733bu_fw rtl8733bu_config"
+CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE_DIR="firmware"
 CONFIG_USB_VIDEO_CLASS=m
 CONFIG_MEDIA_USB_SUPPORT=y
 CONFIG_SPI_SPIDEV=y
diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/Makefile b/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/Makefile
index 1afcef21f..272a595c7 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/Makefile
@@ -15,7 +15,11 @@ ifeq ($(CONFIG_BTCOEX), y)
 endif
 
 ifneq ($(KERNELRELEASE),)
-	obj-m := rtk_btusb.o
+	# Was hardcoded to obj-m, so CONFIG_BT_RTK_HCIBTUSB=y silently still produced
+	# a module. Honour the tristate: built-in lets the RTL8733BU be probed during
+	# kernel init (with the patch/config blobs supplied by CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE)
+	# instead of waiting for userspace to modprobe it.
+	obj-$(CONFIG_BT_RTK_HCIBTUSB) := rtk_btusb.o
 	rtk_btusb-y = rtk_coex.o rtk_misc.o rtk_bt.o
 else
 	PWD := $(shell pwd)
diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/rtk_bt.c b/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/rtk_bt.c
index b00b464da..2edc4888c 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/rtk_bt.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/bluetooth_usb_driver/rtk_bt.c
@@ -2023,6 +2023,39 @@ static int btusb_probe(struct usb_interface *intf,
 	set_bit(HCI_QUIRK_WIDEBAND_SPEECH_SUPPORTED, &hdev->quirks);
 #endif
 
+	/* Force legacy LE advertising.
+	 *
+	 * Two earlier explanations for this quirk were both wrong, so record
+	 * what actually held up:
+	 *
+	 *  - "the firmware rejects Set Ext Scan Rsp/Enable with -22": no. With
+	 *    the quirk off there is no 0x2038 failure. The -22 came from
+	 *    overflowing the 31-byte legacy adv-data limit, not from extended
+	 *    advertising.
+	 *  - "legacy registers but the chip never airs the adv (firmware
+	 *    legacy-TX bug)": also no. The shipping path drives legacy adv
+	 *    straight over a raw HCI socket (LE Set Adv Params/Data/Enable,
+	 *    see tuya_hci.c in the userspace adapter) and phones and nRF both
+	 *    see the FD50 advertisement. The controller airs legacy fine. What
+	 *    looked like "never airs" was mgmt-driven advertising being torn
+	 *    down ~2s after it started, by the rotation timer that
+	 *    hci_schedule_adv_instance_sync() armed for a single unlimited
+	 *    instance. That is fixed in the same series as this quirk.
+	 *
+	 * The quirk stays because it still buys two things. It keeps
+	 * ext_adv_capable() false, so anything that does go through mgmt stays
+	 * on legacy PDUs that plain phone scanners will actually match; and
+	 * hci_cc_le_read_local_features() hangs the 100/120ms adv interval off
+	 * it, without which legacy MGMT_OP_ADD_ADVERTISING would use the 1.28s
+	 * default and make the device slow to discover.
+	 *
+	 * Caveat recorded 2026-07-29, not re-verified since: letting BlueZ use
+	 * extended advertising crashed the app in a glib gdbus race at BLE
+	 * init. If you ever drop this quirk, retest that path.
+	 */
+	set_bit(HCI_QUIRK_BROKEN_EXT_ADV, &hdev->quirks);
+	RTKBT_INFO("HCI_QUIRK_BROKEN_EXT_ADV set (force legacy LE ADV)");
+
 #if HCI_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(3, 7, 1)
 	if (!reset)
 		set_bit(HCI_QUIRK_RESET_ON_CLOSE, &hdev->quirks);
